  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus_sp_aff_tigrinus_en/",
      "html": "<p>A striped Leporinus from the Ventuari river in Venezuela, possibly a still undescribed species. The name Leporinus means \"haresnout\"; tigrinus means \"striped like a tiger\". Suggested common name: Tiger-Leporinus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus_sp_aff_tigrinus_en/",
      "html": "<p>This Leporinus originates from the Ventuari river in Venezuela. Leporinus with a similar coloration are known so far only from the Rio Tapajos and Rio Xingu in Brazil; these were described in 1929 as Leporinus fasciatus tigrinus. Nowadays this subspecies is usually regarded as a full species, e.g. Leporinus tigrinus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus_sp_aff_tigrinus_en/",
      "html": "<p>It is very likely that the fish feeds mainly on plant material.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus_sp_aff_tigrinus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Leporinus are known to be sometimes quarrelsome against their own kind, but these fish are quite peaceful so far.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/leporinus_sp_aff_tigrinus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The double band in front of the dorsal fin and the shining scales on the back are very obvious and seem to be species-specific. One may reckon that the species attains a total length of more than 20 cm, as its close relatives (L. fasciatus, L. affinis, L. y-ophorus) do.</p>"
    }
  ],
